window.docContents[228].resolve({"/#applicative":{"contents":"An applicative functor is a functor that has two additional operations available:  and .\n is the same operator used in , because  in fact inherits from .\n is much like : it allows a function to be used in order to transform the contents of a datatype.\nHowever, with , the function is itself contained in the datatype: .\nHaving the function under the type  allows the  instance to control how the function is applied, while  unconditionally applies a function.\nThe second argument has a type that begins with  to allow the definition of  to short-circuit in cases where the function will never be applied.\n\nThe value of this short-circuiting behavior can be seen in the instance of :\n\n\n\nIn this case, if there is no function for  to apply, then there is no need to compute its argument, so  is never called.\nThe same consideration informs the instance of  for :\n\n\n\nThis short-circuiting behavior depends only on the  or  structures that surround the function, rather than on the function itself.\n\nMonads can be seen as a way of capturing the notion of sequentially executing statements into a pure functional language.\nThe result of one statement can affect which further statements run.\nThis can be seen in the type of : .\nThe first statement's resulting value is an input into a function that computes the next statement to execute.\nSuccessive uses of  are like a sequence of statements in an imperative programming language, and  is powerful enough to implement control structures like conditionals and loops.\n\nFollowing this analogy,  captures function application in a language that has side effects.\nThe arguments to a function in languages like Kotlin or C# are evaluated from left to right.\nSide effects performed by earlier arguments occur before those performed by later arguments.\nA function is not powerful enough to implement custom short-circuiting operators that depend on the specific value of an argument, however.\n\nTypically,  is not invoked directly.\nInstead, the operator <*> is used.\nThis operator wraps its second argument in fun () => ..., simplifying the call site.\nIn other words,  is syntactic sugar for .\n\nThe key feature that allows  to be used with multiple arguments is that a multiple-argument Lean function is really a single-argument function that returns another function that's waiting for the rest of the arguments.\nIn other words, if the first argument to  is awaiting multiple arguments, then the result of the  will be awaiting the rest.\nFor example,  can have the type .\nProviding one argument, , results in the type .\nThis can itself be used with , so  has the type .\n\nNot every functor is applicative.\n is like the built-in product type :\n\n\n\nLike ,  has type .\nThis means that  has type , and a  instance is possible:\n\n\n\nThis instance obeys the  contract.\n\nThe two properties to check are that  =  and that  = .\nThe first property can be checked by just stepping through the evaluation of the left side, and noticing that it evaluates to the right side:\n\n\n\nThe second can be checked by stepping through both sides, and noting that they yield the same result:\n\n\n\n\n\nAttempting to define an  instance, however, does not work so well.\nIt will require a definition of :\n\n\n\n\n\nThere is a value with type  in scope (namely ), and the error message from the underscore suggests that the next step is to use the constructor :\n\n\n\n\n\nUnfortunately, there is no  available.\nBecause  would need to work for all possible types  to define an instance of , this is impossible.\nAfter all, a caller could choose  to be , which has no values at all.\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Functors, Applicative Functors, and Monads","header":"5.2. Applicative Functors","id":"/#applicative"}});